/// A Vulkan device that runs kernels. A program built for `browser/wasm32` has none: `devices` is empty and `open` fails with `NoDevice`.
///
/// A `Device` keeps the pipeline of each kernel it has run and the buffers of its last run. Releasing it waits for its queue to go idle and destroys them, the device and its instance. One `run` uses the device at a time; a second green thread waits.
pub struct Device {
    name: str
    drop {}
}

/// Lists the devices that can run kernels: each has Vulkan 1.2, 64-bit integers in shaders and a compute queue. The list is empty on a machine with no Vulkan driver.
pub fn devices() -> Info[] ! Error {
    []
}

/// Opens the device `devices` lists that has the first `Kind` in the order `Discrete`, `Integrated`, `Virtual`, `Other`, `Software`, and the lowest index among those. Fails with `NoDevice` when the list is empty.
pub fn open() -> Device ! Error {
    fail NoDevice
}

/// Opens the device `devices` lists with `index`, or fails with `NoDevice`.
pub fn open_on(index: i64) -> Device ! Error {
    fail NoDevice
}

/// Returns the name the driver gives the device.
pub fn name(d: Device) -> str {
    d.name
}

/// Runs every invocation of `job` on the device and returns when all have finished.
///
/// `job` is what `launch` gives. `run` copies each slice to the device, runs the kernel's device build once for each index below the job's count, in no defined order, and copies the `mut` slices back. An invocation that indexes outside a slice or divides by zero stops, and `run` fails with `Faulted` after the others have run. A job with a count of zero runs nothing.
pub fn run(d: Device, job: launch) ! Error {
    fail NoDevice
}

/// Device memory that a kernel reads and writes in place, which releases itself when dropped.
///
/// `data` is the elements, all zero at first: a slice over memory the device owns, so the collector neither scans nor moves it. A `launch` given `data`, or a part of it that starts where the device can bind one, runs on the buffer's device with no copy in either direction, and the kernel's writes are in `data` when `run` returns. On another device, on the CPU, and for a part that starts elsewhere, `data` is an ordinary slice. Dropping the `Buffer`, or releasing its device, frees the memory, and a slice copied out of `data` and used afterwards reads or writes unmapped memory, which panics with `invalid memory access`. A `data` slice has no spare capacity, so `push` onto a copy reallocates on the heap and leaves the buffer alone.
pub struct Buffer[T: Plain] {
    /// The elements, whose count is the count that was asked for.
    pub data: T[]
    held: bool
    drop {}
}

/// Allocates `count` zeroed elements of device memory on `d` and returns the buffer that owns them.
///
/// The memory is mapped into the program, cached by the host where the device has such memory. A negative `count` panics. Fails with `NoDevice` when `d` has been released.
pub fn buffer[T: Plain](d: Device, count: i64) -> Buffer[T] ! Error {
    fail NoDevice
}
